Based on the RAA Contents PDS, **Flood** is defined as:

"the covering of normally dry land by water that has escaped or been released from the normal confines of:
• any lake, or any river, creek or other natural watercourse, whether or not altered or modified; or
• any reservoir, canal or dam." [RAA Contents PDS, page 87]

**Exclusions from the Flood definition:**
Flood excludes water from Actions of the Sea, except where caused by:
• a Storm Surge if it occurs at the same time your Insured Property is damaged by a Storm; or
• a Tsunami.

Flood also excludes a mixture of water from Actions of the Sea and water from any other source. [RAA Contents PDS, page 88]

**Additional exclusions applying to Flood cover (Section 12):**
Even where the above definition of Flood is met, cover does not apply to loss or damage:
• to any part of the Home that is not fully built;
• to tennis courts;
• to a wharf, jetty, pontoon or sea wall;
• to water in a tank, swimming pool or spa, including cleaning costs of mud/debris from these;
• to swimming pools or spas and their covers or liners (except permanently attached hard lid style pool covers);
• to paint where there is no structural damage caused by Flood to other parts of the Home;
• caused by underground (hydrostatic) water;
• to a glasshouse or greenhouse;
• to unsealed paths or driveways (e.g., gravel or loose materials);
• caused by Flood arising from deliberate or Malicious Damage to, or destruction of, a reservoir or dam; and/or
• to paths, driveways (and other items listed) if they were not structurally sound or reasonably maintained prior to the loss [RAA Contents PDS, page 50]

The excerpt cuts off mid-list on page 50, so there may be additional items beyond "a path" and "a driveway" in that final exclusion bullet — the available excerpts do not show the complete list.
